983 1145 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10(1)-3 \\\\ 10(1)-3 & 10(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-10(2)+5(1)^{2}-3(1)-2 \\\\ 10(2)(1)-3(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-10)\\cdot 20-7\\cdot 7}[\\begin{aligned}20 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{249}[\\begin{aligned}-498 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380985 13194139536049 true] [17592186380984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380986 13194139536049 true] [17592186380984 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536049 true] [17592186380984 1041 "field-1" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380984 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380985 13194139536049 true] [17592186380984 1110 #uuid "a6d73a87-cba0-53d8-8575-d59070c26e9e" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380985 1034 "1" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380985 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536049 true] [17592186380985 1111 #uuid "50c72475-85fc-52ee-b4c7-80ae4f6092b1" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380986 1034 "0" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536049 true] [17592186380986 1111 #uuid "daa7556e-343d-53f4-9901-35b878f3e8c5" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380987 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380988 13194139536049 true] [17592186380987 1089 "q-257190" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380987 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536049 true] [17592186380987 1100 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}+2xy-3x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.375$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380987 1113 #uuid "1c14fc02-43f0-5353-b2b2-d653536058c5" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380987 1145 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}+2xy-3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}+2xy-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2x+2y-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-2x+2y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-2x+2y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6y-2 & 6x+2 \\\\ 6x+2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y-2 & 6x+2 \\\\ 6x+2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2x+2y-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y-2 & 6x+2 \\\\ 6x+2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2x+2y-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(2)-2 & 6(1)+2 \\\\ 6(1)+2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6(1)(2)-2(1)+2(2)-3 \\\\ 3(1)^{2}+2(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}10 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{10\\cdot 0-8\\cdot 8}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{64}[\\begin{aligned}-40 \\\\ -38\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.375 \\\\ 1.406\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380989 13194139536049 true] [17592186380988 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536049 true] [17592186380988 1041 "field-1" 13194139536049 true] [17592186380988 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380989 13194139536049 true] [17592186380988 1110 #uuid "5fc47041-c54b-5c7a-8e27-09d460849126" 1319413953